  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 356 AM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nTuesday, as the upper ridge begins to amplify north, up the West \nCoast into Canada, the upper trough begins to dive south over the \nAtlantic Coast states. A closed off low is expected to develop \nwithin the trough and move east across Ontario with a nearby surface \nlow that will slide into Quebec. It will be this low that will drag \na cold front under northwest flow that stretches from the Upper \nPlains, through the Midwest, and into the Ohio Valley. This cold \nfront will bring a chance for severe weather as it brings forcing \nand winds energy into an area with warm temperatures and lots of \nmoisture. \n\nYesterday while this system was in reach of the global models, shear \nat all levels and instability levels looked very concerning. Low \nlevel winds were very backed. It looked more concerning than today's \ndata in the global models and CAMs. Instability remains high with \nMLCAPE values over 2,500 J/kg. MUCAPE could be over 3,500 J/kg. Deep \nlayer shear has dropped some with 0-6 km values now near 30-40 \nknots, but directional shear isn't looking as good. Pooling moisture \nahead of the front could push precipitable water values back above \n2\".\n\nMost of the model guidance is having this system push through as a \nsingle line with a developed cold pool pushing the line out with \nstraight-line winds being the main threat, or maybe two waves will \npush through with one pushing through early in the day as the system \nsurges ahead and another line coming through close to the front \nlater in the afternoon or evening. If supercells were able to \ndevelop ahead of the line, large 2+ inch hail and tornadoes would \nbecome more likely. Low level lapse rates are also more stable today \nthan yesterday, dropping DCAPE values from over 1,200 J/kg to 800 \nJ/kg. This could also make it harder to get all the wind energy to \nthe ground, reducing confidence in 65+ knot winds. The uncertainty \nin storm mode and lack of a clear threat led SPC to pull the \n\"Enhanced\" farther east to only the Bluegrass region in our area.  \n\nHeat could also be a concern as highs currently are forecast to \nclimb back into the upper 80s to low 90s. With dew points in the \n70s, reaching the upper 70s along the western edge of the CWA, heat \nindices could reach to 105 in Ohio and surrounding counties. \nDepending on how early the expected system moves in. This could \nimpact high temperatures. \n\nBehind the front, surface high pressure will bring CAA and cooler \ntemperatures with highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s through Friday \nbefore the high passes to the east and WAA returns, brining mid 80s \nto low 90s back for the weekend. Dry weather is expect through the \nend of the workweek with low-end rain chances returning \naround the weekend.",
